From 6da5477ba68b08fdfa54d6c4f267bb0bf0789872 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: opencode <opencode@localhost>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 19:18:27 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Add blockwise signed Hadamard transform for SYCL (ternary
 weights)

The existing ggml_sycl_op_fwht serves KV-cache rotation: its n is a head
dimension, a power of two in 64..512, and it transforms a whole row in one
N-wide butterfly. Ternary weight dequant needs a different thing -- the
rotated axis is the input width (5120 / 6144 / 17408 for Ternary-Bonsai-2-27B),
which is not a power of two, so a single N-wide WHT is not defined. The model
declares a block size (1024) and a random +-1 sign vector, and the transform is
blockwise at that size.

This adds sycl_fwht_blocked(): per (row, block), y = H_BLOCK . (signs .* x),
normalised. It uses the Kronecker factorisation H.1024 = H.512 (x) H.2, so a
block is two independent 512 transforms and peak usage stays at 512/WARP_SIZE
= 32 registers per lane -- the figure the existing 512 path already uses --
rather than block/WARP_SIZE = 64, which spills at WARP_SIZE 16. x is read
twice; that is the deliberate cost.

The entry point refuses anything it cannot serve, rather than falling through
to an ordinary mat-mul: a transform that is silently not applied is the
failure mode this exists to prevent.

Validated on an Intel Arc Pro B70 at all three shapes the model needs --
(BLOCK,NBLOCKS) = (1024,5), (1024,6), (1024,17) -- against a CPU reference,
worst |d| = 4.77e-07, using a real 1024-block of prism.hadamard.sign_values
from Ternary-Bonsai-2-27B-PTQ1_0.gguf. Six injected faults (dropped signs,
swapped halves, reversed butterfly, removed sub-group permute, dropped scale,
wrong block offset) are each caught; three of them were missed before the test's
reference buffer was correctly sized, which is why that fix is part of this.

The ggml-facing adapter is compiled out under FWHT_BLOCKED_NO_GGML_WRAPPER so
the kernel can be tested without linking all of libggml. Not yet wired into
the MUL_MAT dispatch: the sign vector has no path from GGUF metadata to the
op yet.
